00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.530
And of course we will have questions at the
end.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.230
So, what are these things? the variation services
and APIs?

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:37.280
Set of URL based, RESTful, programming interfaces
for accessing and working with variation data

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.280
of NCBI.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.740
They rely on a new variation representation
called SPDI.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:46.620
Which stands for sequence: position: deletion:
insertion.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:52.190
They are used internally at NCBI dbSNP and
ClinVar.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:57.570
It's how we manage to deal with these large
numbers of variants at our presence in both

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:58.980
of those resources.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:04.480
We will talk a little bit about the variant
model for the next few minutes.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.720
Simple representation that uses a sequence
ID.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.000
The location and the alleles.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.930
The deletion - take something out.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.950
Insertion, put someting back in.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.210
Represent with precise coordinates.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.000
There easier for variant normalization.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.970
We will talk about what we I mean by that
in a few minutes.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.840
They are designed to be machine-readable.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.850
Although, people can figure out what they
mean as well.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.640
I'll explain why they are more machine-readable
than people readable in just a minute.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.970
They are amenable to high-performance computation,
which is why we use them internally at NCBI

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:39.970
for sequence variant processing.

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.920
You can represent both nucleotide and protein
sequence variants.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:50.190
At the bottom of the slide, there are some
URLs that will take you to web document about

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:58.070
SPDI notation, which is helpful, but much
more detailed is the preprint available on

00:02:58.070 --> 00:02:59.070
bioRxiv.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.180
There is a link to it there on the bottom
of the slide.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.180
biorxiv.org/content/biorxiv/early/2019/01/31/537449.full.pdf

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.130
If you want to know more about this variant
model, that's the place to go to find out

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.180
all about it.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:14.400
Here is an example of a variant that is shown
in SPDI notation.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.500
This is a linear form of this.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:21.701
As you can see also, on the slide, there is
a structured format a JSON JavaScript object

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:25.340
format for the SPDI, which is what the APIs
are going to return to you.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:26.920
Basically, you have a sequence ID.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.490
This is a reference sequence for chr8.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:36.660
A position in that reference sequence, then
there is a base that is deleted and a base

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.360
that is inserted.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:44.100
Notice that the SPDI variant models does not
require that you have the deleted base there.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.760
You can just use the length of the base.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.410
And that brings me to the next point on the
slide.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:55.020
Which is that SPDI uses -this is the part
that makes it a little tricky to read for

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.020
people.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.580
It uses a zero base coordinate system.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.760
It is an inter-based one.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:09.099
Basically, the insertion/deletion starts between
bases 1981972 and 1981973.

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:11.819
The space between those bases.

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:21.889
The G that is being deleted is the G at position
1981973, in the one-based sequence coordinate

00:04:21.889 --> 00:04:22.889
system.

00:04:22.889 --> 00:04:26.949
And so, the advantage of this from a computational
point of view, is that you can actually represent

00:04:26.949 --> 00:04:31.550
an insertion with the variant length of 0.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:37.259
Here is the corresponding example of SPDI
notation for a protein variant.

00:04:37.259 --> 00:04:43.439
Again, written both ways with deleted sequence
base, or the deletion length.

00:04:43.439 --> 00:04:44.990
Again.

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:48.270
You can get this as an object, a structured
format.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:49.599
From the API.

00:04:49.599 --> 00:04:55.229
We use SPDI behind the scenes in the variant
service behind the scene in the variations

00:04:55.229 --> 00:05:00.200
services, the variation databases and resources
that NCBI.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.949
If you want to see them live and in person
in action, here's a place you can see that.

00:05:03.949 --> 00:05:07.759
The variant search in ClinVar uses SPDI.

00:05:07.759 --> 00:05:13.680
If you search ClinVar with HGVS you'll see
these kinds of things.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.960
It you know that you might have a variant
that is not in ClinVar.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:21.440
Then it will map you to dbSNP.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:27.159
Or there might be an equivalent variant that
will link you to that variant.

00:05:27.159 --> 00:05:30.699
So what can you do with these variation services?

00:05:30.699 --> 00:05:32.150
Sort of three things you could do.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:33.930
You can normalize your variant.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:40.029
That means finding the equivalent one in NCBI
dbSNP and ClinVar variants.

00:05:40.029 --> 00:05:42.860
You can convert formats.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:46.410
If you have a SPDI you can get the HGVS and
vice versa.

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:51.939
You can do all these kinds of things to standardize
the way your variants are shown.

00:05:51.939 --> 00:05:56.430
You can correct for left and right shifting
for insertions and deletions.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:01.249
We will talk a little bit more about that
when we talk about contextual alleles.

00:06:01.249 --> 00:06:06.180
We can transform your data into right shifted
HGVS or left shifted VCF.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.270
That is the preferred way of writing them.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:12.749
The useful thing you can do is to remap the
sequence variant.

00:06:12.749 --> 00:06:17.159
That uses our alignment database system.

00:06:17.159 --> 00:06:20.060
To allow you to map variants between assemblies.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.770
Between various sequences that are in there.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.919
It also gives you access to annotations.

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:30.159
Because, you can access the snp records in
a structured format.

00:06:30.159 --> 00:06:34.139
Those are going to include functional consequences,
allele frequency, and clinical significance

00:06:34.139 --> 00:06:38.460
and all kinds of other important information
about the variant.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:45.289
A couple of terms that we are going to come
across today, that I am going to mention.

00:06:45.289 --> 00:06:49.369
One of them is the idea of a contextual allele.

00:06:49.369 --> 00:06:51.340
These are alphabetical on the slide.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:56.800
So, contextual allele is a unique, normalized
representation that is corrected for something

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.689
called over precision.

00:06:59.689 --> 00:07:01.879
Over precision is pretty easy to understand.

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:08.639
If you look at a portion of DNA, for example
that has a low complexity region or repeat

00:07:08.639 --> 00:07:09.810
in it.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.400
If you look at these examples shown at the
bottom of the slide, you can think about a

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:14.400
deletion.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.409
You see G AGA repeats there.

00:07:16.409 --> 00:07:20.270
If I remove one of those GA's, it doesn't
matter which one I remove, I'm going to end

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.129
up with the same sequence, result.

00:07:23.129 --> 00:07:30.319
So really, over specifying which one of those
are used is what is called over precision.

00:07:30.319 --> 00:07:33.720
The problem with that is, you can give rise
to things that look like different variants,

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.740
when they are in fact, not different variants.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:39.080
It is one of the things that SPDI has been
helpful for.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.770
Corrects for those kind of things.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:47.409
The other thing that I want to mention is
this canonical representative allele or representative

00:07:47.409 --> 00:07:48.990
record that you can see.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:52.139
This is a really useful thing to do with the
variation services.

00:07:52.139 --> 00:07:57.020
It will allow you to map to a particular sequence
model.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.969
The thing that we call a canonical allele
is usually based on the latest genomic assembly,

00:08:00.969 --> 00:08:02.120
which is GRCh38.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:10.830
So if you want to map an allele on a different
sequence to the standard allele in GRCh38,

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:14.159
you can do that using the variation services.

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:19.039
This is just a slide that shows you how you
can do these kinds of things in sort of a

00:08:19.039 --> 00:08:20.039
schematic.

00:08:20.039 --> 00:08:22.319
You've got all these different variants in
different formats.

00:08:22.319 --> 00:08:27.830
You onvert them to a SPDI . In particular,
you want to try to get the contextual alleles.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:32.760
Those are going to normalize for overly precision,
overly precise variants.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:40.560
And you can correct all of your problems,
make your VCF and HGVS compliant with sort

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.150
of the standard.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:49.390
And then, you could also remap these, using
the alignment database system, into various

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.730
genome builds and things like that.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:59.430
Here are a couple of simple examples of sort
of normalization, or standardizing your variants.

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:05.240
So, at the top of the slide, I have an HGVS
name for a variant.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:06.240
NC_000007.14:g.117548634_117548635insTT

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:07.400
It's written as insertion.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:10.080
So insert Two T's.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:16.520
I can convert that using the SPDI services
to a SPDI contextual allele.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:20.500
This is an unambiguous representation of this
particular variant.

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:21.500
NC_000007.14:117548628:TTTTTTT:TTTTTTTTT

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:27.770
Then, I can convert that back into HGVS, on
all these different reference sequences.

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:28.770
NG_016465.4:g.87851_87852dup
NC_000007.13:g.117188688_117188689dup

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:29.770
NC_000007.14:g.117548634_117548635dup

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:32.870
And the preference is to make this representation
a duplication rather than insertion.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:40.060
They you can see the correct way, or the preferred
way of writing these, as a dup.

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.250
This is a more complicated example.

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:49.480
First of all, you will notice that the HGVS
expression contains a GenBank accession number

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:52.950
here, that's a BRCA2 mRNA.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:53.950
U43746.1:c.2472_2477del

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:58.060
Because the alignment database has some of
the INSDC or GenBank

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:02.050
in it aligned to all of the reference sequences
and things like that.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:03.790
This is written as a deletion.

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:10.480
And it is actually left shifted HGVS, which
means that the deletion is shown sort of at

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:14.770
the left-hand side of where this variant should
be.

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:17.960
This is a problem because this is a repeat
sequence.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:22.700
I can turn this into a unique representation
called us SPDI contextual allele.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:23.700
U43746.1:2699:AAATGAAAAT:AAATT

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.870
And I can normalize the HGVS that way.

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:26.870
U43746.1:c.2476_2481delGAAAAT

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:32.460
That I will get back to a right shifted, showing
the right hand end, of that insertion/deletion.

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:37.000
I can course take back all the contextual
alleles and all of the HGVS expressions.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:40.600
I am mapping it to chromosome 13 here in this
one.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:41.600
NM_000059.3:c.2476_2481delGAAAAT
NG_012772.3:g.26352_26357delGAAAAT

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:42.600
NC_000013.10:g.32910968_32910973delGAAAAT
NC_000013.11:g.32336831_32336836delGAAAAT

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:43.600
Okay.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.010
So, had you do this?

